# Grade 6: teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'6-1-1': dict(
  saint="Jerome: the scholar who said that ignorance of Scripture is ignorance of Christ.",
  tryit=["Read 2 Timothy 3:16 and 2 Peter 1:21.", "Write who the authors are, and who the Author is.", "Explain inspiration in your own words."],
  saintStory="Jerome was born around 347 in Dalmatia and studied in Rome. He was brilliant, and quick-tempered. After a dream in which he felt accused of loving classical writers more than Christ, he gave himself to Scripture. He spent years as a hermit in the Syrian desert, where he taught himself Hebrew.\nIn Rome, Pope Damasus asked him to produce a reliable Latin Bible. Jerome later settled in Bethlehem, near the cave of Jesus’ birth, and worked for over thirty years translating the Old Testament from Hebrew and revising the New. His translation, the Vulgate, was the Church’s standard Bible for more than a thousand years.\nHe wrote, “Ignorance of Scripture is ignorance of Christ.” He died in 420 in Bethlehem.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a Bible. Ask, “Who wrote this?” Take answers (Moses, Paul, God). Say, “All of those answers are partly right. Today we find out how.”",
    teach=["Read 2 Timothy 3:16 and 2 Peter 1:21. Say: “‘All Scripture is inspired by God’ and ‘men moved by the Holy Spirit spoke from God.’ Inspiration means God is the principal author of the Bible. He used real human authors, who wrote in their own language, style and setting, and he guided them so that they wrote all that he wanted written and nothing more. Think of a musician and an instrument, except that the human authors were free and active, not instruments only.”", "Say: “Because God is the author, Scripture teaches truth without error: the truth God wanted put in writing for our salvation. This is called inerrancy. Because people are the authors, we must pay attention to who was writing, to whom, in what time and in what form. The Bible is both fully divine and fully human, a little like Jesus, who is both God and man. This is why Dei Verbum, the Second Vatican Council’s document on Scripture, speaks of God’s word in human words.”", "Say: “The Catholic Bible has 73 books: 46 in the Old Testament and 27 in the New. The Church recognized which books belong in the canon, the official list, by the Spirit’s guidance, at councils in the late fourth century. The Church did not invent the Bible, but she discerned which writings were inspired. Catholic Bibles include seven Old Testament books that many Protestant Bibles omit: Tobit, Judith, Wisdom, Sirach, Baruch, and 1 and 2 Maccabees. Jerome’s Latin translation is the Vulgate.”"],
    ask=["What does inspiration mean? (God is the principal author; he guided human authors.)", "How many books are in the Catholic Bible? (73.)", "Why do we have to read the Bible with attention to the human authors? (They wrote in a time, place and form.)"],
    practice=["Pairs read 2 Timothy 3:16 and 2 Peter 1:21 aloud.", "On a sheet, draw two columns: “God’s part” and “The human author’s part.”", "Fill each with what the verses and the lesson say.", "Write one sentence explaining inspiration to a younger child."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, open our hearts to your word.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Find the table of contents in a Bible and count the books with someone.”")),

'6-1-2': dict(
  saint="Augustine: the teacher who learned to read Scripture in the light of Christ.",
  tryit=["Sort passages by literary form.", "Name the form.", "Say how it changes what you look for."],
  saintStory="Augustine was born in 354 in North Africa. As a young man he was clever, ambitious and restless. He found the Bible rough and unsophisticated compared to the writers he admired, and he turned away from it for years.\nIn Milan, he heard the bishop Ambrose preach, and was surprised to learn that texts that had seemed foolish to him had deeper meanings. Ambrose taught him that Old Testament stories could be read as pointing to Christ. Augustine began to read the Bible again, and in 386, in a garden, he heard a child’s voice saying, “Take and read.” He opened Romans 13 and was converted.\nAs bishop of Hippo he wrote a guide to interpreting Scripture. He said that the New Testament is hidden in the Old, and the Old is revealed in the New.",
  script=dict(
    open="Read three short pieces aloud with no labels: a line of a poem, a line from a news report, a line from a joke. Ask, “How did you know how to take each?” Say, “The Bible has the same variety.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Bible is a library of many literary forms. There is history (1 and 2 Kings), law (Leviticus), poetry and song (Psalms), wisdom sayings (Proverbs), prophecy (Isaiah), Gospel (Mark), letters (Romans), and apocalyptic writing (Revelation). We read each according to its form. We would not read a poem as a newspaper report. Asking what the author intended, in that form, is the literal sense, the foundation of all interpretation.”", "Say: “Parables are short stories with a point. Genesis 1 is a theological account of creation, using the language of a week and a pattern of days to teach that God made everything good and gave us the Sabbath. It is not a science textbook. History in the Bible is true history told for faith, and the authors sometimes arrange events to teach. The Church warns us not to pit Scripture against science, since truth cannot contradict truth.”", "Say: “Beyond the literal sense, the Church speaks of three spiritual senses. The allegorical sense sees how events point to Christ: the crossing of the Red Sea points to Baptism. The moral sense shows how to act: Israel’s wandering teaches us to trust God. The anagogical sense points to our final destiny in heaven: Jerusalem as a sign of heaven. The literal sense comes first, and the spiritual senses are built upon it.”"],
    ask=["Name three literary forms in the Bible. (History, poetry, prophecy, Gospel, letter.)", "What is the literal sense? (What the human author intended to say in that form.)", "What are the three spiritual senses? (Allegorical, moral, anagogical.)"],
    practice=["Give small groups passages: Psalm 23, Luke 15:11-32, Proverbs 10:1-5, 1 Kings 3:16-28, 1 Corinthians 13, Genesis 1:1-5.", "Each group names the form and says what to look for in that form.", "Pick one passage and find its literal sense in one sentence, then one spiritual sense.", "Share with the class."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, as we read your word, help us to understand it.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Before you read a Bible passage this week, ask what kind of writing it is.”")),

'6-1-3': dict(
  saint="Irenaeus: the bishop who pointed to the apostles’ teaching, handed down.",
  tryit=["Draw the three-legged stool.", "Write on each leg what it adds.", "Explain why one leg alone would fall."],
  saintStory="Irenaeus was born around 130 in Asia Minor. As a boy, he heard Polycarp of Smyrna, who had known the apostle John. Irenaeus later moved to Lyon, in Gaul, where he became bishop after the previous bishop was martyred.\nTeachers were spreading false ideas called Gnosticism, claiming secret knowledge that was hidden from ordinary Christians. Irenaeus wrote a long work, Against Heresies, to answer them. He said the truth is not secret. It was given to the apostles, and handed on openly in the Church through the bishops, who could trace their succession back to them. He pointed to the Church in Rome as an example.\nHe died around 202. Irenaeus is a witness that the teaching we have is the teaching the apostles gave.",
  script=dict(
    open="Play a game of whispers: pass a short sentence down a line of six students. Compare the first and last. Ask, “How could the Church be sure that Jesus’ teaching was handed down without being changed?”",
    teach=["Say: “Jesus did not write a book. He taught, healed, and formed the apostles, and he sent them: ‘Go therefore and make disciples… teaching them to observe all that I have commanded you.’ (Matthew 28:19-20) The apostles passed on his teaching by preaching, by example and by writing. Some of that was written down by inspired authors and became the New Testament. The rest continued as living practice and teaching.”", "Say: “Sacred Tradition is the living handing-on of the Gospel from the apostles, through the Church, by the guidance of the Holy Spirit. Sacred Scripture and Sacred Tradition flow from the same source, God’s revelation, and the Church accepts both with equal reverence. Paul wrote, ‘Stand firm and hold to the traditions which you were taught by us, either by word of mouth or by letter.’ (2 Thessalonians 2:15) Tradition is not a set of old customs. It is the Gospel, living in the Church.”", "Say: “The Magisterium is the Church’s teaching office: the Pope and the bishops in union with him. Jesus gave Peter and the apostles the task of teaching with authority, and promised, ‘He who hears you hears me.’ (Luke 10:16) The Magisterium is not above God’s word, but serves it, teaching only what has been handed on. Together the three work like a three-legged stool: Scripture, Tradition and the Magisterium. Take one away and the stool falls.”"],
    ask=["What is Sacred Tradition? (The living handing-on of the Gospel from the apostles.)", "What is the Magisterium? (The Church’s teaching office: the Pope and bishops.)", "How do Scripture, Tradition and Magisterium fit? (They work together, and the Magisterium serves the word.)"],
    practice=["Draw a three-legged stool. Label each leg.", "Under each, write what it gives and a verse: Scripture (2 Timothy 3:16), Tradition (2 Thessalonians 2:15), Magisterium (Luke 10:16).", "On the seat, write “the Gospel, whole and unchanged.”", "Explain to a partner what would happen if one leg were missing."],
    close="Pray the Creed together slowly. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask someone where they learned the faith, and who taught them.”")),

'6-2-1': dict(
  saint="Kateri Tekakwitha: a young woman who saw God in the forest and the river.",
  tryit=["Go outside or look at a window.", "Write what you see that God made.", "Write a thank-you line for each."],
  saintStory="Kateri Tekakwitha was born in 1656 in a Mohawk village in what is now New York State. Her mother was an Algonquin Christian, and her father a Mohawk chief. When Kateri was four, smallpox killed her parents and little brother, and left her face scarred and her eyesight weak.\nShe lived with her uncle. When Jesuit missionaries visited, she listened to them, and she was baptized at twenty, on Easter Sunday, 1676. Some in her village disliked her faith, and she was threatened. She walked about two hundred miles to a Christian native village near Montreal.\nThere she prayed in the woods, where she set up crosses on trees, and she helped the sick and old. She died at twenty-four, in 1680. Kateri is the patron of ecology. She praised God in what God had made.",
  script=dict(
    open="Take the class outside, or to a window. Ask them to be silent for one minute and notice five things made by God. Come back and share.",
    teach=["Read Genesis 1:1-5, 26-31. Say: “‘In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.’ The Bible opens with God making everything out of nothing, simply by his word. The Church calls this creation ex nihilo, from nothing. God did not need anything to work with, and he did not make the world because he was lonely or needed it. He made it freely, out of love.”", "Say: “Seven times Genesis says ‘God saw that it was good.’ After humans were made, ‘it was very good.’ Creation is good, because God made it. Matter is good. The body is good. Human beings are made in the image of God, which means we can think, choose and love, and we are made for relationship. God gave us dominion, which means care, not abuse. Genesis 2:15 says the man was placed in the garden ‘to till it and keep it.’ We are stewards of creation.”", "Say: “The Genesis account is not a scientific account of how the world developed. It is a theological teaching about who made it, why, and what it means. The Church teaches that faith and science cannot truly contradict, since God is the author of both truth in nature and truth in Scripture. The Sabbath day, the seventh, is when God rested, and sets a pattern for us: work and then worship. Psalm 19:1: ‘The heavens declare the glory of God.’”"],
    ask=["What does creation from nothing mean? (God made everything by his word without any pre-existing material.)", "What does ‘made in the image of God’ mean? (We can think, choose and love.)", "What does being a steward mean? (Taking care of creation as God’s gift.)"],
    practice=["Pairs look out the window or go outside for five minutes.", "On a sheet, list ten things God made that you saw or can think of.", "Beside each, write one thing it shows about God (for example, “mountain: God is strong”).", "Write a thank-you psalm line, for example, “Thank you, God, for ___.”"],
    close="Pray Psalm 8:3-5 or the verse of Psalm 19:1. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Do one thing this week to care for creation.”")),

'6-2-2': dict(
  saint="Mary: the woman who said yes where Eve had said no.",
  tryit=["Compare Eve’s story and Mary’s story.", "Fill in the two columns.", "Say what changed."],
  saintStory="Mary was a young woman of Nazareth, engaged to Joseph, when the angel Gabriel appeared to her. He greeted her as “full of grace” and told her she would conceive and bear a son, Jesus, who would be great, and whose kingdom would have no end.\nMary asked, “How can this be, since I have no husband?” The angel said that the Holy Spirit would come upon her, and that nothing is impossible with God. Mary answered, “Behold, I am the handmaid of the Lord; let it be to me according to your word.”\nThe Fathers of the Church, such as Irenaeus, saw a parallel. Eve, in Eden, listened to the serpent and disobeyed. Mary, in Nazareth, listened to the angel and obeyed. Through Eve came the fall, and through Mary, the Savior. They call Mary the new Eve.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write the word ‘forbidden’ on the board and ask, “Why does a locked door make you want to open it?” Take three quick answers. Say, “The Bible has the oldest story of this feeling.”",
    teach=["Read Genesis 2:15-17 and 3:1-13 in sections. Say: “God put Adam and Eve in the garden, gave them everything, and set one limit: not to eat from the tree of the knowledge of good and evil. The serpent, who stands for Satan, questioned God’s word. ‘Did God say…?’ He suggested that God was holding back, and told them they would be like God. Eve took the fruit, and Adam ate. Their eyes were opened, but only to see that they were naked and ashamed. They hid. When God asked, Adam blamed Eve, and Eve blamed the serpent.”", "Say: “This is the story of the fall, and the Church calls the result original sin. The first humans, by their free choice, turned away from trusting God. Because of it, human nature was wounded, and we all inherit it: we are weakened in our will, drawn to evil, subject to suffering and death. Original sin is not a personal fault we commit, but a condition we are born into. Baptism removes it, but its effects remain. But God did not abandon them. In Genesis 3:15, he promises that the offspring of the woman will crush the serpent’s head. The Church calls this the first Gospel.”", "Say: “Mary’s story answers Eve’s. Eve listened to the serpent and disobeyed; Mary listened to the angel and said yes. Through a woman came the fall; through a woman, the Savior. That is why the Church calls Mary the new Eve. And Jesus is the new Adam: Paul writes, ‘As in Adam all die, so also in Christ shall all be made alive.’ (1 Corinthians 15:22) Where Adam failed in a garden, Jesus obeyed in another garden, Gethsemane.”"],
    ask=["What is original sin? (The wounded condition of human nature we inherit from the first sin.)", "What did God promise in Genesis 3:15? (A Savior born of a woman who would defeat the serpent.)", "Why is Mary called the new Eve? (She obeyed God where Eve disobeyed.)"],
    practice=["Draw a chart with two columns: Eve and Mary.", "Fill in rows: Who spoke to her? What did he ask or say? What did she answer? What happened next?", "At the bottom, add a row for Adam and Jesus.", "Write one sentence that sums up what changed."],
    close="Pray the Hail Mary slowly. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Next time you hear the Hail Mary, think of Eve and Mary.”")),

'6-2-3': dict(
  saint="Noah: a righteous man who trusted God when no one else did.",
  tryit=["Tell the story of the flood in five pictures.", "Add the sign of the rainbow.", "Say what each story shows about sin and mercy."],
  saintStory="After Adam and Eve, human wickedness spread. Cain, their son, was jealous of his brother Abel, because God accepted Abel’s offering. God warned Cain that sin was crouching at the door, but Cain killed Abel. When God asked where Abel was, Cain said, “Am I my brother’s keeper?” God said the blood cried out from the ground.\nGenerations later, the earth was full of violence. But Noah was a righteous man who walked with God. God told him to build an ark. It took years, and he was surely mocked. When the flood came, Noah, his family and the animals were saved. After the waters went down, God made a covenant with Noah and set his bow in the clouds as a sign that he would never again destroy the earth by flood.\nLater at Babel, people built a tower to make a name for themselves, and God scattered them. Pride divides; trust in God saves.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a picture of a rainbow. Ask, “What do you think when you see one? Do you know where it comes from in the Bible?”",
    teach=["Read Genesis 4:1-16 in summary. Say: “Cain and Abel each brought an offering. God accepted Abel’s but not Cain’s. Cain was angry, and God said, ‘If you do well, will you not be accepted? And if you do not do well, sin is crouching at the door; its desire is for you, but you must master it.’ Cain did not listen. Sin was growing: from disobedience to murder in one generation. Yet even then God protected Cain with a mark. God’s mercy meets sin.”", "Read Genesis 6:5-9:17 in summary. Say: “Violence filled the earth. God was grieved. But Noah found favor. He built the ark as God said. Eight people were saved through water. The Church sees this as a sign of Baptism (1 Peter 3:20-21). After the flood, God made a covenant: never again would a flood destroy all flesh. The rainbow is the sign. A covenant is a sacred bond between God and people. This one was with all creation.”", "Read Genesis 11:1-9 in summary. Say: “At Babel, people said, ‘Let us build ourselves a city and a tower with its top in the heavens, and let us make a name for ourselves.’ They wanted to reach heaven by themselves. God confused their language and scattered them. The story shows that pride divides people. At Pentecost this is reversed: the Spirit comes, and each person hears the apostles in their own language. What pride scatters, the Spirit gathers.”"],
    ask=["What did God say to Cain about sin? (It is crouching at the door; master it.)", "What is the sign of the covenant with Noah? (The rainbow.)", "How does Pentecost answer Babel? (The Spirit gathers people of every language.)"],
    practice=["Fold a sheet into six boxes.", "Draw the flood story in five pictures: the warning, building the ark, the flood, the dove, the rainbow.", "In the sixth, draw Babel.", "Under each, write one line on what it shows about sin or God’s mercy."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, you keep your promises. Thank you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look for a rainbow and remember God’s covenant.”")),

'6-3-1': dict(
  saint="Abraham: our father in faith, who left his home without knowing where he was going.",
  tryit=["Mark Abraham’s journey on a map.", "Label the three promises.", "Write what trust cost him."],
  saintStory="Abraham, first named Abram, lived in Ur, a city in Mesopotamia, with his wife Sarai. They were old and had no children. God said to him, “Go from your country and your kindred and your father’s house to the land that I will show you. I will make of you a great nation.” Abram went, at seventy-five, not knowing where.\nHe traveled to Canaan, and God promised the land to his descendants. Later God took him outside at night and said, “Look toward heaven, and number the stars, if you are able. So shall your descendants be.” Abram believed the Lord. God changed their names to Abraham and Sarah, and when Abraham was a hundred, Isaac was born.\nWhen Isaac was a boy, God tested Abraham by asking him to offer Isaac. Abraham obeyed, trusting God, and at the last moment an angel stopped him. Paul calls Abraham the father of all who believe.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say, “I’m going to ask you to pack and leave tomorrow for a place I won’t tell you. Would you go? What would you need to believe about me?” Let several answer. “Abraham did exactly this.”",
    teach=["Read Genesis 12:1-4. Say: “God called Abram: ‘Go from your country… to the land that I will show you.’ The call came with three promises. Land: a home for his descendants. Descendants: a great nation. Blessing: ‘In you all the families of the earth shall be blessed.’ And Abram went. The letter to the Hebrews says, ‘By faith Abraham obeyed when he was called to go out to a place which he was to receive as an inheritance; and he went out, not knowing where he was to go.’ (Hebrews 11:8)”", "Say: “Years went by and no child came. Abraham was ninety-nine and Sarah ninety when God promised again, and Sarah laughed. But the child came. God made a covenant with Abraham, sealed with circumcision, a sign in the flesh. A covenant is more than a contract: it makes the people family. Later, in Genesis 22, God asked Abraham to offer Isaac. This was a terrible test. Abraham trusted that God would keep his promise, even if he had to raise Isaac from the dead. God provided a ram. The Church sees here a sign of the Father offering his own Son.”", "Say: “Abraham is a model of faith. Faith is not just believing facts. It is trusting God and acting on it, even when we can’t see the way. Paul writes, ‘Abraham believed God, and it was reckoned to him as righteousness.’ (Romans 4:3) We pray in the Eucharistic Prayer: ‘our father Abraham.’ The promise to Abraham is fulfilled in Jesus. Through Christ, all nations are blessed.”"],
    ask=["What three promises did God make to Abraham? (Land, descendants, blessing for all nations.)", "Why is Abraham a model of faith? (He trusted God and acted without seeing the way.)", "What does Genesis 22 foreshadow? (The Father offering his Son.)"],
    practice=["Give each pair a blank map of the Middle East.", "Mark the route: Ur to Haran to Canaan (Shechem, Bethel, Hebron) and Egypt.", "Label the three promises.", "On the back, write: “What did trust cost Abraham? What does it cost me?”"],
    close="Pray: “Lord, give us faith like Abraham’s.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Do one thing this week that takes trust.”")),

'6-3-2': dict(
  saint="Jacob: the man who wrestled with God and received a new name.",
  tryit=["Make a timeline of Jacob’s life.", "Mark where God meets him.", "Explain what changed him."],
  saintStory="Jacob was the younger twin son of Isaac and Rebekah. He was clever and ambitious. When his older brother Esau came in hungry, Jacob persuaded him to trade his birthright for a bowl of stew. Later, with his mother’s help, he tricked his blind father Isaac into giving him the blessing meant for Esau. Furious, Esau planned to kill him, so Jacob fled.\nOn the way, he slept with a stone for a pillow, and dreamed of a ladder reaching to heaven, with angels going up and down. God promised him the land and descendants. Jacob woke and said, “Surely the Lord is in this place, and I did not know it.” He went to work for his uncle Laban, was himself tricked, and married Leah and Rachel. After twenty years, he returned.\nThe night before he met Esau, he wrestled with a man until dawn. The man touched his hip, and said, “Your name shall no more be called Jacob, but Israel, for you have striven with God.” Jacob walked away limping, blessed. His twelve sons became the twelve tribes of Israel.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Have you ever had to say sorry to someone you wronged and were afraid of how they’d respond? Jacob did.”",
    teach=["Say: “Isaac was Abraham’s promised son. His son Jacob had a complicated life. He cheated his brother Esau out of the birthright and the blessing. God did not choose Jacob because he was perfect. God’s promise continued through flawed people. It is grace, not merit. Jacob fled and slept at Bethel, where he had the dream of the ladder. God was with him, though he’d done wrong. The Church sees the ladder as a sign of Christ, who joins heaven and earth. (John 1:51)”", "Say: “Years later, Jacob was heading home and feared Esau. Genesis 32 says he wrestled with a man all night. At dawn, he said, ‘I will not let you go, unless you bless me.’ The man gave him a new name: Israel, which means ‘he who strives with God.’ He limped afterward. It was a night of struggle and prayer, and he was changed. The next day, Esau ran to meet him and embraced him. They were reconciled.”", "Say: “Jacob had twelve sons, who became the twelve tribes of Israel. One of them, Judah, is the ancestor of David and of Jesus. Jacob’s story teaches that God keeps his promises despite human weakness, that we can wrestle with God in prayer, and that God can change a person. Notice: Jesus chose twelve apostles, a sign of the new Israel.”"],
    ask=["Why did God’s promise continue through Jacob? (It depends on God’s grace, not Jacob’s goodness.)", "What new name did Jacob receive? (Israel.)", "What did the twelve sons become? (The twelve tribes of Israel.)"],
    practice=["Draw a timeline of Jacob’s life on a long sheet: birthright, blessing, Bethel, Laban, marriage, return, wrestling, reconciliation.", "Mark with a star each place where God meets Jacob.", "Circle the turning point.", "Write two sentences: What changed Jacob? What helps change me?"],
    close="Pray: “Lord, I will not let you go until you bless me.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray about one hard thing tonight.”")),

'6-3-3': dict(
  saint="Joseph, son of Jacob: sold by his brothers, he forgave them and saved them.",
  tryit=["Trace the stages of Joseph’s story.", "Mark each fall and rise.", "Write what he said to his brothers."],
  saintStory="Joseph was Jacob’s favorite son, and his brothers resented him. He had dreams that he would rule over them. One day, when he was seventeen, they threw him into a pit, then sold him to merchants who took him to Egypt. They told their father that a wild animal had killed him.\nIn Egypt, Joseph served in Potiphar’s house, but he was falsely accused and thrown in prison. There he interpreted the dreams of two officials. Two years later, when Pharaoh had troubling dreams, Joseph was called. He explained that seven good years would be followed by seven years of famine, and advised storing grain. Pharaoh made him second in command of Egypt.\nWhen the famine came, his brothers traveled to Egypt to buy grain, not knowing who he was. After testing them, Joseph revealed himself, weeping. He said, “You meant evil against me; but God meant it for good, to bring it about that many people should be kept alive.” The whole family moved to Egypt.",
  script=dict(
    open="Draw a staircase going down and then up on the board. Say, “Joseph’s life goes down, down, down, and then up. Write what you think happens at each step.” Let students predict from what they know of the story.",
    teach=["Read Genesis 37 in summary. Say: “Joseph’s brothers hated him: he was their father’s favorite, with the special coat, and he told them his dreams. They plotted to kill him, but sold him instead. A young man was alone in a foreign land. The story shows jealousy at work, and how sin spreads: lying to their father, and living with the guilt for years.”", "Read Genesis 39-41 in summary. Say: “In Egypt, Joseph was faithful. When Potiphar’s wife tried to seduce him, he refused, saying, ‘How can I do this great wickedness, and sin against God?’ He was thrown in prison unjustly. But ‘the Lord was with Joseph.’ In time, he was brought before Pharaoh, interpreted his dreams, and was made governor. It took thirteen years. Joseph never gave up trusting God.”", "Read Genesis 45:1-8 and 50:15-21 in summary. Say: “When his brothers came, Joseph could have taken revenge. Instead he wept, and said, ‘Do not be distressed… for God sent me before you to preserve life.’ After their father died, the brothers feared he would punish them. Joseph said, ‘Do not fear… you meant evil against me; but God meant it for good.’ Joseph is a type, or foreshadowing, of Jesus: loved by his father, betrayed by his brothers for silver, handed over to foreigners, raised to glory, and became the savior of those who wronged him.”"],
    ask=["Why did the brothers sell Joseph? (Jealousy.)", "How did Joseph respond to his brothers? (He forgave them, and saw God’s plan.)", "How is Joseph like Jesus? (Betrayed by his brothers, raised up, and saved them.)"],
    practice=["Draw a line with ups and downs.", "Mark the stages: favorite son, pit, slavery, prison, governor, reunion.", "Under each “down,” write how Joseph stayed faithful.", "At the end, write what Joseph said to his brothers in your own words."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, help me to forgive as Joseph did.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Forgive one person who hurt you.”")),

'6-4-1': dict(
  saint="Moses: a shy shepherd who spoke with God at a burning bush.",
  tryit=["Read Exodus 3:1-15 aloud in parts.", "List what God says about himself.", "Say what God asked Moses to do."],
  saintStory="Moses was born in Egypt, when Pharaoh had ordered every Hebrew baby boy to be thrown into the Nile. His mother hid him for three months, then placed him in a basket among the reeds. Pharaoh’s daughter found him and raised him as her son. Moses grew up in the palace, but he knew he was a Hebrew.\nOne day he killed an Egyptian who was beating a Hebrew slave, and fled to Midian, where he became a shepherd and married Zipporah. For forty years he lived quietly. One day, he saw a bush burning but not consumed. A voice said, “Moses, Moses! Take off your shoes, for the place where you are standing is holy ground.”\nGod said, “I have seen the affliction of my people… I will send you to Pharaoh.” Moses said, “Who am I?” and “I am slow of speech.” God promised, “I will be with you.” And when Moses asked God’s name, God answered, “I AM WHO I AM.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Light a candle and set it in front of the class. Ask, “What would you do if you saw a bush on fire that did not burn up?” Then say, “Moses had been a shepherd for forty years, and was about to meet God.”",
    teach=["Read Exodus 3:1-10. Say: “God said, ‘I have seen the affliction of my people… and I have heard their cry… I know their sufferings, and I have come down to deliver them.’ Notice the verbs: seen, heard, know, come down. God is not distant. He sees suffering and acts. But he chooses to act through a person: ‘Come, I will send you to Pharaoh.’ God often saves through people he calls.”", "Say: “Moses had excuses. ‘Who am I?’ God said, ‘I will be with you.’ ‘What is your name?’ God said, ‘I AM WHO I AM.’ This is the sacred name, written YHWH, which Jews do not pronounce out of reverence. It means God is, and is always present, the source of all being. ‘Please send someone else.’ Even Moses’ reluctance did not stop God. He gave his brother Aaron to speak for him. Notice that God’s call is never only a command. It comes with a promise to help.”", "Say: “The burning bush also teaches us about holiness. ‘Take off your shoes, for the place where you are standing is holy ground.’ God’s presence makes a place holy and calls for reverence. Moses hid his face. The Church sees in the bush that burned without being consumed a sign of Mary, who carried God without being harmed. For us, this teaches reverence in church, at Mass, and before the Blessed Sacrament.”"],
    ask=["What four verbs does God use of himself in Exodus 3:7-8? (Seen, heard, know, come down.)", "What does God’s name ‘I AM’ mean? (God is, the source of all being, always present.)", "What did God promise Moses? (I will be with you.)"],
    practice=["Read Exodus 3:1-15 aloud with parts: narrator, God, Moses.", "On a sheet, list everything the passage says about who God is.", "In a second list, write Moses’ excuses and God’s answers.", "Write one thing God might be asking of you, and one excuse you might make."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, you are with us. Take away our fear.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Take off your shoes, in your heart, when you pray.”")),

'6-4-2': dict(
  saint="Miriam: the sister of Moses who led the women in song at the sea.",
  tryit=["Put the ten plagues and the Passover in order.", "Match each Passover element to the Eucharist.", "Write a song line for the Red Sea."],
  saintStory="Miriam was the older sister of Moses and Aaron. As a girl, she watched from among the reeds when her baby brother’s basket was put in the Nile. When Pharaoh’s daughter found him, Miriam stepped forward and asked, “Shall I go and call you a nurse from the Hebrew women?” She brought her own mother, who nursed Moses.\nYears later, after the Israelites crossed the Red Sea and Pharaoh’s army was swept away, Miriam, called a prophetess, took a tambourine, and all the women followed her with tambourines and dancing. She sang, “Sing to the Lord, for he has triumphed gloriously; the horse and his rider he has thrown into the sea.”\nShe traveled with the people through the wilderness, and died at Kadesh. Her song is the oldest in the Bible and is sung again at the Easter Vigil.",
  script=dict(
    open="Set a plate of flatbread or crackers on the table, with no yeast. Say, “Imagine eating a meal standing up, shoes on, ready to run. What kind of meal is that?” Take guesses before reading Exodus 12.",
    teach=["Read Exodus 11-12 in summary. Say: “God sent ten plagues on Egypt, but Pharaoh would not let the people go. The tenth was the death of the firstborn. God told Israel to take a lamb without blemish, kill it, and put its blood on the doorposts. ‘When I see the blood, I will pass over you.’ They were to eat the lamb roasted, with unleavened bread and bitter herbs, dressed for travel. This was the first Passover. That night, Pharaoh let them go.”", "Read Exodus 14. Say: “Then Pharaoh changed his mind and chased them. The people were trapped between the army and the sea, and were afraid. Moses said, ‘Fear not, stand firm, and see the salvation of the Lord.’ The sea parted. Israel walked through on dry ground. The waters returned and drowned the Egyptians. Israel sang. God had saved them. They passed from slavery to freedom through water.”", "Say: “The Passover is celebrated every year by the Jewish people. The Church sees in it a sign of what Jesus did. Jesus is the Lamb of God (John 1:29), whose blood saves us. His Last Supper was a Passover meal. On the cross, he was the sacrificial Lamb. At the Mass, we receive the Lamb. The Red Sea is a sign of Baptism: we pass through water from slavery to sin to freedom. The Easter Vigil reads this story every year.”"],
    ask=["What saved the Israelites in Egypt? (The blood of the lamb on the doorposts.)", "What did God do at the sea? (He parted it, and Israel passed through on dry ground.)", "What does the Red Sea foreshadow? (Baptism.)"],
    practice=["Set out cards with the events: plagues, lamb and blood, unleavened bread, midnight, Exodus, chased, Red Sea, song.", "Put them in order.", "In a second column, match each to what it points to: lamb (Jesus), blood (his sacrifice), bread (Eucharist), sea (Baptism).", "Write a short song line like Miriam’s in thanksgiving for being saved."],
    close="Sing or recite “Behold the Lamb of God.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask your family what Easter means to you.”")),

'6-4-3': dict(
  saint="Bezalel: the craftsman God filled with his Spirit to build the meeting tent.",
  tryit=["Sort the Ten Commandments into two tablets.", "Write the matching Beatitude or New Law teaching.", "Say why God gave the Law after freeing Israel."],
  saintStory="After the Israelites left Egypt, they camped at the foot of Mount Sinai. God called Moses up the mountain and gave him the Ten Commandments, written on two stone tablets. He also gave instructions for a tent of meeting, the tabernacle, where God would dwell among his people, and for the ark of the covenant to hold the tablets.\nGod said to Moses, “I have called by name Bezalel the son of Uri… and I have filled him with the Spirit of God, with ability and intelligence, with knowledge and all craftsmanship.” Bezalel worked in gold, silver, bronze, wood and stone. With another craftsman, Oholiab, he made the ark, the lampstand, the altar and the priestly garments.\nHe is the first person in the Bible said to be filled with the Spirit. His story shows that God gives gifts for worship, and that beauty serves holiness.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “When someone gives you rules, do you ever feel like they are against you? Think about a game: do rules make it worse or make it possible?”",
    teach=["Read Exodus 19-20 in summary. Say: “Three months after leaving Egypt, Israel came to Mount Sinai. God spoke from fire and cloud. He offered a covenant: ‘If you will obey my voice and keep my covenant, you shall be my own possession among all peoples.’ The people answered, ‘All that the Lord has spoken we will do.’ Notice the order. God first saved them, and then gave the Law. The Ten Commandments begin, ‘I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt.’ The Law is the response of a free people, not the price of freedom.”", "Say: “The Ten Commandments, or Decalogue, are written on two tablets. The first three are about love of God: no other gods, do not take his name in vain, keep the Sabbath holy. The other seven are about love of neighbor: honor your father and mother, do not kill, do not commit adultery, do not steal, do not bear false witness, do not covet your neighbor’s wife, do not covet your neighbor’s goods. Jesus summarized them: love God and love your neighbor.”", "Say: “Soon after, while Moses was on the mountain, the people made a golden calf and worshiped it. This was a breaking of the covenant. Moses interceded for them, and God forgave. God then renewed the covenant. The tabernacle, with its beautiful furnishings made by Bezalel, was the sign that God would dwell with them. The Law is a gift: it shows the path of life, and it protects freedom and love. Jesus said, ‘If you love me, you will keep my commandments.’ (John 14:15)”"],
    ask=["Why did God give the Law after the Exodus? (It was the response of a free people to God who saved them.)", "How are the two tablets divided? (Love of God and love of neighbor.)", "What happened at the golden calf? (Israel broke the covenant; God forgave.)"],
    practice=["Draw two tablets on a sheet.", "On the first, write the first three commandments; on the second, the other seven.", "Beside each, write one way to keep it today.", "At the bottom, write what Jesus says is the summary."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, your law is a lamp for my feet.” (Psalm 119:105.) Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Learn the Ten Commandments in order.”")),

'6-5-1': dict(
  saint="David: a shepherd boy who became king, sinned deeply, and repented.",
  tryit=["Tell David’s story in four scenes.", "Mark where he trusted God and where he fell.", "Read Psalm 51:1-4."],
  saintStory="After the time of the judges, the people asked Samuel for a king like other nations. God chose Saul, but Saul disobeyed, and God sent Samuel to anoint another. Samuel went to Jesse in Bethlehem. Jesse’s seven older sons passed before him, but the Lord said, “Man looks on the outward appearance, but the Lord looks on the heart.” The youngest, David, was out with the sheep. Samuel anointed him.\nDavid later faced the giant Goliath, armed only with a sling and trust in God. “You come to me with a sword and spear, but I come to you in the name of the Lord of hosts.” He became king, united the tribes and made Jerusalem the capital. God promised that his house and throne would last forever.\nBut David also sinned gravely. He took Bathsheba, the wife of Uriah, and had Uriah killed. The prophet Nathan told him a parable, and said, “You are the man.” David said, “I have sinned against the Lord.” He repented, and wrote Psalm 51.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a slingshot or a small stone. Ask, “Who has the advantage in a fight: a giant or a boy with a sling? Why?” Take a vote and listen to the arguments before turning to the story.",
    teach=["Read 1 Samuel 16:1-13 in summary. Say: “Samuel was sent to anoint a new king from Jesse’s sons. Samuel looked at the tallest and said, ‘Surely the Lord’s anointed is before him.’ But God said, ‘Do not look on his appearance… for the Lord sees not as man sees; man looks on the outward appearance, but the Lord looks on the heart.’ The youngest, David, was brought in from the fields. ‘Arise, anoint him; for this is he.’ And the Spirit of the Lord came mightily upon David from that day.”", "Read 1 Samuel 17 in summary. Say: “Goliath was a champion of the Philistines, over nine feet tall. For forty days he mocked Israel’s army, and nobody dared fight him. David, sent by his father with food for his brothers, heard him and said, ‘Who is this uncircumcised Philistine, that he should defy the armies of the living God?’ Saul tried to give him armor, but it was too heavy. David took his staff, five smooth stones and his sling. ‘The Lord who delivered me from the paw of the lion and from the paw of the bear will deliver me.’ One stone brought down the giant.”", "Read 2 Samuel 11-12 in summary. Say: “David became king and was successful. But one night he saw Bathsheba and took her, and then arranged for her husband to be killed in battle. Nathan the prophet confronted him with a story of a rich man who stole a poor man’s lamb. David was furious. Nathan said, ‘You are the man.’ David said, ‘I have sinned against the Lord.’ He was forgiven, but there were consequences. Psalm 51 is his prayer of repentance: ‘Create in me a clean heart, O God.’ David is great not because he never sinned, but because he repented. And God’s promise to David is fulfilled in Jesus, the Son of David.”"],
    ask=["How does God choose? (He looks at the heart.)", "What gave David strength against Goliath? (Trust in the Lord.)", "What did David do when Nathan confronted him? (He admitted his sin and repented.)"],
    practice=["Fold a sheet in four.", "Draw four scenes: anointing, Goliath, king, Nathan.", "Mark with a heart where David trusted God, and a cross where he fell.", "Read Psalm 51:1-4 aloud and underline a line you could pray."],
    close="Pray Psalm 51:10: “Create in me a clean heart, O God.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray Psalm 51 before Confession.”")),

'6-5-2': dict(
  saint="Solomon: the king who asked for wisdom and built the Temple.",
  tryit=["Read 1 Kings 3:5-14.", "Write what Solomon asked and what God gave.", "Describe what the Temple was for."],
  saintStory="When David was old, his son Solomon became king. Soon afterward, at Gibeon, God appeared to him in a dream and said, “Ask what I shall give you.” Solomon answered, “Give your servant an understanding mind to govern your people, that I may discern between good and evil.” God was pleased that he had not asked for long life, riches or victory. He gave him a wise and discerning heart, and added riches and honor besides.\nSolomon’s wisdom became known. Two women came to him, each claiming to be the mother of a baby. He called for a sword to divide the child. One woman agreed; the other cried out to give the baby to the first. Solomon knew who the mother was.\nHe built the Temple in Jerusalem, taking seven years. But later in life, Solomon married many foreign wives and let them draw him to worship their gods. The kingdom split after he died.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a picture of a famous cathedral or temple. Say, “Why do people build huge buildings for God? What do you think is the point?” Write answers on the board and compare them with Solomon’s reasons later.",
    teach=["Read 1 Kings 3:5-14. Say: “God said, ‘Ask what I shall give you.’ Solomon was a young king, and he knew his limits. ‘I am but a little child; I do not know how to go out or come in.’ He asked for an understanding heart. God said, ‘Because you have asked this, and have not asked for yourself long life or riches… behold, I give you a wise and discerning mind.’ And God added what he had not asked: riches and honor. This is what Jesus taught: ‘Seek first his kingdom and his righteousness, and all these things shall be yours as well.’ (Matthew 6:33)”", "Read 1 Kings 6 and 8 in summary. Say: “Solomon built the Temple on Mount Moriah in Jerusalem. It was the first permanent house for God’s presence in Israel, with the ark of the covenant in the Holy of Holies. At its dedication a cloud filled the house, and Solomon prayed, ‘Will God indeed dwell on the earth? Behold, heaven and the highest heaven cannot contain you; how much less this house which I have built!’ The Temple was the center of Israel’s worship, where sacrifices were offered. It was a sign of God’s nearness.”", "Say: “But Solomon did not stay faithful. He taxed heavily and allied himself with foreign nations through marriages. His wives led him to worship idols. After his death, the kingdom divided into two: Israel in the north and Judah in the south. The story warns that gifts from God can be lost when we stop following him. Wisdom is a gift, and also a habit. The Temple was destroyed later, but Jesus is the true Temple (John 2:19-21), and we are living stones of the Temple of the Spirit.”"],
    ask=["What did Solomon ask for? (An understanding heart.)", "What was the Temple for? (God’s dwelling and Israel’s worship.)", "What happened to Solomon in later life? (He turned to idols, and the kingdom split.)"],
    practice=["Draw a sheet with two columns: “What Solomon asked” and “What God gave.”", "Fill them in from 1 Kings 3.", "Draw a simple plan of the Temple: porch, Holy Place, Holy of Holies. Label the ark.", "Write one sentence: “If God said, ‘Ask,’ I would ask for ___ because ___.”"],
    close="Pray the prayer of Solomon: “Give your servant an understanding heart.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask God for wisdom before a decision.”")),

'6-5-3': dict(
  saint="Elijah: the prophet who stood against King Ahab and heard God in a whisper.",
  tryit=["Match each prophet to a message.", "Say who they warned and what they promised.", "Choose a prophet and write a one-line summary."],
  saintStory="Elijah was a prophet in the northern kingdom of Israel, in the time of King Ahab and his wife Jezebel, who promoted the worship of the false god Baal. Elijah told Ahab there would be no rain until he said so. For three years there was a drought.\nThen Elijah challenged the prophets of Baal to a contest on Mount Carmel. Each side would prepare a sacrifice, and the god who answered with fire would be God. The four hundred and fifty prophets of Baal called from morning to evening, and nothing happened. Elijah drenched his altar with water and prayed a simple prayer. Fire fell from heaven and consumed the sacrifice. The people cried, “The Lord, he is God.”\nJezebel threatened him, and Elijah fled into the desert, wishing to die. An angel fed him, and he walked forty days to Mount Horeb. There God was not in the wind, the earthquake or the fire, but in a still small voice. Elijah was later taken up in a chariot of fire.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Is it easy or hard to tell someone a truth they don’t want to hear? Who does it for a living? In Israel, God sent prophets.”",
    teach=["Say: “A prophet is not mainly a fortune-teller. A prophet is someone who speaks for God. The prophets reminded Israel of the covenant, called kings and people back to God, warned of the consequences of unfaithfulness, and also gave hope for the future. Often they were not welcome. Their main message was: Be faithful to God, and care for the poor. Many suffered for speaking.”", "Say: “Prophets before the exile: Elijah and Elisha fought idolatry. Amos spoke against injustice: ‘Let justice roll down like waters.’ Hosea compared God’s love to a faithful husband. Isaiah saw God’s holiness and the coming Messiah (Isaiah 7:14, 9:6, 53). Jeremiah warned of the exile. After the exile, Ezekiel promised a new heart and Daniel spoke of the Son of Man. The Church reads the prophets as pointing to Jesus. John the Baptist was the last of them and the first to point out Christ.”", "Say: “Elijah’s story at Horeb shows how God speaks. Elijah expected God in power, in wind, earthquake and fire. But God came in a ‘still small voice.’ God often speaks quietly, in the silence of prayer. Elijah appears again with Moses at the Transfiguration, representing the Prophets and the Law, speaking with Jesus about his Exodus in Jerusalem. The prophets prepare us to hear him.”"],
    ask=["What did the prophets do? (Spoke for God, called people back to the covenant, gave hope.)", "How did God speak to Elijah? (In a still small voice.)", "Who appears with Jesus at the Transfiguration? (Moses and Elijah.)"],
    practice=["Prepare cards for six prophets: Elijah, Amos, Hosea, Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel. On other cards, write their messages.", "Teams match each prophet to a message and a Bible reference.", "Choose one and write a short summary: whom did he warn, and what did he promise?", "Share with the class."],
    close="Pray in silence for one minute, listening for the still small voice. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Spend a few minutes in silence each day.”")),

'6-6-1': dict(
  saint="Jeremiah: the prophet who wept over Jerusalem and promised a new covenant.",
  tryit=["Tell the story of the exile in five steps.", "Mark why it happened.", "Read Jeremiah 29:11."],
  saintStory="Jeremiah was a young man in a priestly family when God called him. “Before I formed you in the womb I knew you.” Jeremiah said, “Ah, Lord God! Behold, I do not know how to speak, for I am only a youth.” The Lord said, “Do not say, ‘I am only a youth.’ I am with you to deliver you.”\nFor over forty years he warned Judah: if they did not turn back to God and stop their injustice and idolatry, Jerusalem would fall. People did not want to hear it. He was beaten, put in stocks, thrown into a cistern, and imprisoned. He was called the weeping prophet because he grieved for his people.\nIn 587 BC, Babylon destroyed Jerusalem and the Temple. Many were taken to Babylon in exile. Jeremiah wrote them a letter: “Build houses and live in them… seek the welfare of the city… For I know the plans I have for you, plans for welfare and not for evil, to give you a future and a hope.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Read aloud the first line of Psalm 137: “By the waters of Babylon, there we sat down and wept.” Then sit in silence for a few seconds. Say, “Imagine being forced from your home. What would you miss first?”",
    teach=["Say: “After Solomon, the kingdom split. The northern kingdom, Israel, fell to Assyria in 722 BC. The southern kingdom, Judah, lasted longer, but kept breaking the covenant with idolatry and injustice. The prophets warned. In 587 BC, Babylon under Nebuchadnezzar captured Jerusalem, burned the Temple and took many into exile. This was the greatest crisis in Israel’s history. They had lost the land, the Temple and the Davidic king.”", "Read Psalm 137:1-4. Say: “‘By the waters of Babylon, there we sat down and wept, when we remembered Zion.’ In exile, the people asked, ‘How shall we sing the Lord’s song in a foreign land?’ Some said God had abandoned them. But the prophets said the exile was a consequence of sin, not the end. Jeremiah told them to settle and pray, because the exile would last seventy years. Ezekiel saw visions of dry bones coming to life, a promise of new life for Israel.”", "Read Jeremiah 31:31-34. Say: “Jeremiah gave a promise: ‘Behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, when I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel… I will put my law within them, and I will write it upon their hearts; and I will be their God, and they shall be my people.’ At the Last Supper, Jesus said, ‘This cup is the new covenant in my blood.’ This is the promise fulfilled. The exile taught Israel that God was with them even in a foreign land.”"],
    ask=["Why did the exile happen? (Israel broke the covenant; Babylon conquered.)", "What did Jeremiah tell the exiles to do? (Settle, pray, and seek the welfare of the city.)", "What did Jeremiah promise? (A new covenant written on the heart.)"],
    practice=["Draw a five-step comic: the warning, the siege, the fall of Jerusalem, the exile, the hope.", "Under each, write one line.", "Circle the step where God’s promise gives hope.", "Read Jeremiah 29:11 and write it on a card to keep."],
    close="Pray Jeremiah 29:11 together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for people far from home.”")),

'6-6-2': dict(
  saint="Nehemiah: a cupbearer who rebuilt Jerusalem’s walls in fifty-two days.",
  tryit=["Plan a rebuilding project.", "List the obstacles.", "Add a prayer for each."],
  saintStory="Nehemiah was a Jewish man who served as cupbearer to the Persian king Artaxerxes, far from Jerusalem. One day, his brother arrived with news: the survivors in Jerusalem were in great trouble, and the wall of the city was broken down and its gates burned. Nehemiah sat down and wept. For days he fasted and prayed.\nWhen the king noticed his sad face, Nehemiah told him, and asked to go and rebuild. The king agreed, and sent him with letters and timber. In Jerusalem, Nehemiah inspected the walls by night, and then called the people to rebuild. Families worked on the section by their own houses. Enemies mocked them, then threatened to attack. Nehemiah set guards, and “each of the builders had his sword girded at his side while he built.”\nThe wall was finished in fifty-two days. Then Ezra read the Law to the people, and they renewed the covenant.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a photograph of a city rebuilt after a disaster. Say, “Who rebuilds? What do they need first?” Take answers and write the needs on the board, then see how Nehemiah met them.",
    teach=["Say: “In 539 BC, Persia conquered Babylon. King Cyrus let the Jews return home. About fifty thousand went back and began rebuilding the Temple. It was completed in 515 BC, but it was smaller than Solomon’s, and some older people wept. The people were poor. The city had no walls. Later Ezra, a priest and scribe, returned to teach the Law, and Nehemiah, a governor, came to rebuild the city walls.”", "Read Nehemiah 1-2 and 4 in summary. Say: “Nehemiah’s response was to pray first. He prayed for months. Then he acted, with courage, planning and leadership. When opposed, ‘we prayed to our God and set a guard.’ That is a good pattern: pray as if everything depends on God, and work as if it depends on you. Notice that Nehemiah did not try to do it alone. Everyone had a section.”", "Read Nehemiah 8 in summary. Say: “When the wall was complete, the people gathered, and Ezra read the Law from morning till midday. The people wept as they heard it, but Nehemiah said, ‘Do not be grieved, for the joy of the Lord is your strength.’ They celebrated the feast of Booths. The return from exile was not a complete restoration. There was no king, and they were under Persian rule. The prophets looked forward to something greater, the coming of the Messiah. But Israel was a community centered on the Law and the Temple.”"],
    ask=["Who let the Jews return? (Cyrus of Persia.)", "What did Nehemiah do first? (He prayed and fasted.)", "What happened when Ezra read the Law? (The people wept, then rejoiced.)"],
    practice=["Pick a real project to rebuild: a clean schoolyard, a food drive, a card for a lonely neighbor.", "On a sheet, list the steps and who does each (like families on each part of the wall).", "List two obstacles and write a short prayer for each.", "Make a one-sentence promise to start."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, strengthen the work of our hands.” (Psalm 90:17.) Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Take the first step of your project.”")),

'6-6-3': dict(
  saint="Ambrose: a governor who became a bishop, and who taught his people to sing the psalms.",
  tryit=["Sort psalms by type.", "Write a lament, a praise and a thanks line.", "Choose a proverb to live by."],
  saintStory="Ambrose was born around 340 into a Roman family, and became governor of northern Italy, based in Milan. When the bishop of Milan died, there was a dispute about who should be chosen. Ambrose went to the church to calm the crowd. A child cried out, “Ambrose for bishop!” and the crowd took it up. He was not even baptized. He tried to refuse, but was baptized, ordained and consecrated bishop within a week.\nHe studied Scripture and the Fathers with energy. He preached so powerfully that Augustine came to hear him. When Arians threatened to take a church in Milan, he and his people barricaded themselves inside, singing hymns and psalms to pass the time. He wrote hymns that are still sung.\nAmbrose confronted the emperor Theodosius over a massacre, and required him to do public penance. He died in 397. He loved the psalms: “In the psalms we meet the whole of human life.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If you could only use ten words to talk to God about your feelings today, what would you say? Maybe it’s anger, joy, fear, thanks. The Psalms have a prayer for each.”",
    teach=["Say: “The book of Psalms has 150 prayers and songs, many written by David. They were Israel’s hymnbook, and Jesus prayed them. The Church prays them in the Mass and in the Liturgy of the Hours. The psalms cover every human feeling. Types include praise (Psalm 100), thanksgiving (Psalm 138), lament (Psalm 22), trust (Psalm 23), repentance (Psalm 51), and royal psalms about the king, which point to Christ (Psalm 2, 110).”", "Say: “Lament psalms show that it’s allowed to bring our pain and even anger to God. Psalm 22 begins, ‘My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?’ Jesus prayed it on the cross. Notice that most laments turn to trust by the end. The Psalms teach us to pray honestly. The Church also reads them as speaking of Christ: Psalm 22 describes the crucifixion, and Psalm 110 speaks of Christ’s priesthood.”", "Say: “The Wisdom books are Proverbs, Job, Ecclesiastes, Song of Songs, Wisdom and Sirach. They help us live well. Proverbs gives short sayings: ‘The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom.’ Job asks why the innocent suffer; God does not explain, but shows himself. Ecclesiastes asks what is the point of life. Wisdom literature teaches that true wisdom is knowing God and living in right relationship with him, others and creation.”"],
    ask=["How many psalms are there? (150.)", "Which psalm did Jesus pray on the cross? (Psalm 22.)", "What does the fear of the Lord mean? (Reverent trust and awe before God.)"],
    practice=["Give each group a handful of psalms to look at: 8, 23, 51, 100, 130, 139, 22.", "Sort them into types: praise, trust, repentance, lament, thanks.", "Each student writes a one-line psalm of their own in one of those types.", "Choose one proverb from Proverbs 10-15 to live by this week."],
    close="Pray Psalm 100 together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray one psalm each night this week.”")),

'6-7-1': dict(
  saint="John the Baptist: the last prophet, who pointed to the Lamb of God.",
  tryit=["Collect messianic prophecies.", "Match each to its fulfillment in Jesus.", "Explain what was promised."],
  saintStory="John the Baptist was the son of the priest Zechariah and Elizabeth, born when they were old, after an angel announced his birth. Zechariah was struck mute for doubting, and when the baby was born and named John, his tongue was loosed, and he prophesied.\nJohn lived in the desert, wearing camel hair and eating locusts and wild honey. Crowds came to him at the Jordan, where he called them to repent and be baptized. He said, “I baptize you with water; but he who is mightier than I is coming.” When Jesus came, John said, “Behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world!”\nHe baptized Jesus, then told his own disciples to follow him. “He must increase, but I must decrease.” John told King Herod that it was wrong to take his brother’s wife, and was imprisoned and beheaded.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write on the board three questions someone might ask on meeting a stranger who says he is a king. Have the class decide which three questions would be the hardest to fake, and then compare with the Messiah’s signs.",
    teach=["Say: “Messiah means ‘anointed one’; in Greek, Christ. Kings, priests and prophets were anointed with oil. Israel looked for one anointed by God to save them. The prophets gave promises about him. He would be born of the line of David (2 Samuel 7:12-16, Isaiah 11:1). He would be born in Bethlehem (Micah 5:2). A virgin would conceive and bear a son, Immanuel (Isaiah 7:14). He would be called Wonderful Counselor, Mighty God, Prince of Peace (Isaiah 9:6).”", "Say: “Other promises were stranger. Isaiah 53 describes a servant who is ‘despised and rejected,’ ‘wounded for our transgressions,’ who ‘bore the sin of many.’ Psalm 22 describes a righteous sufferer whose hands and feet are pierced and whose garments are divided. Zechariah 9:9 describes a king riding a donkey. Daniel 7 has a ‘son of man’ receiving an everlasting kingdom. Together, they portray a Messiah who is both glorious king and suffering servant. It took Jesus’ life to show how those fit.”", "Say: “After the exile came about four hundred years with no prophets. Then John the Baptist appeared. Jesus said that he was Elijah who was to come. He prepared the way. The Gospels often say events happened ‘to fulfill what was spoken by the prophet.’ After the Resurrection, Jesus walked with two disciples on the road to Emmaus and ‘beginning with Moses and all the prophets, he interpreted to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning himself.’ (Luke 24:27) That is how the Church reads the Old Testament: as pointing to Christ.”"],
    ask=["What does Messiah mean? (Anointed one.)", "Name two prophecies about the Messiah. (Born in Bethlehem; a virgin will conceive; suffering servant.)", "What did Jesus do on the road to Emmaus? (Explained the Scriptures that spoke about himself.)"],
    practice=["Make a two-column chart: Prophecy and Fulfillment.", "Fill in rows: Micah 5:2 / Matthew 2:1; Isaiah 7:14 / Matthew 1:22-23; Zechariah 9:9 / Matthew 21:1-5; Psalm 22:18 / John 19:23-24; Isaiah 53:5 / 1 Peter 2:24.", "Add one that you find yourself.", "Choose the one that surprises you most, and say why."],
    close="Pray the Benedictus (Luke 1:68-79) or: “Blessed be the Lord, the God of Israel.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read one prophecy and its fulfillment with your family.”")),

'6-7-2': dict(
  saint="Justin Martyr: a philosopher who found in the Old Testament the Christ he had been searching for.",
  tryit=["Match each type to Christ.", "Say how the type is like and unlike him.", "Draw one pair."],
  saintStory="Justin was born around the year 100 in Samaria, to pagan parents. He studied one philosophy after another, looking for the truth: Stoic, Pythagorean, Platonic. One day, walking by the sea, he met an old man who talked to him about the prophets, who were older than the philosophers and had foretold Christ. Justin said that a fire was kindled in his soul.\nHe became a Christian but kept wearing the philosopher’s cloak, and opened a school in Rome. He wrote to the emperor to defend Christians against false charges. In his First Apology he gave the earliest detailed description of the Mass outside the New Testament: the readings, the homily, the prayers, the bread and wine, and the collection for the poor.\nHe also argued that the Old Testament pointed to Christ. In about 165, he was arrested with some of his students, and beheaded for refusing to sacrifice to the gods.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a map with a road sign pointing to a city. Ask, “Is the sign the same as the city? What is the sign for?” Say, “The Old Testament is full of signs pointing to Christ.”",
    teach=["Say: “Typology is the Church’s way of reading the Old Testament. A type is a person, event or thing in the Old Testament that points ahead to something greater in Christ, called the antitype. The type has its own meaning in its own time, and it is also a preview. The Church Fathers, such as Justin, loved typology. St. Augustine said, ‘The New Testament lies hidden in the Old, and the Old is made clear in the New.’”", "Say: “Here are some types. Adam is a type of Christ, the new Adam (Romans 5). Abel, the innocent man killed, is a type of Christ. Noah’s ark prefigures the Church, and the flood prefigures Baptism. Isaac carrying wood for his own sacrifice prefigures Jesus carrying the cross (Genesis 22). Joseph, betrayed by his brothers and raised up, is a type of Christ. Moses, the lawgiver and liberator, prefigures Christ; the Passover lamb prefigures the Lamb of God; the manna prefigures the Eucharist (John 6); the bronze serpent lifted up prefigures the cross (John 3:14); David prefigures the Messianic king; Jonah, three days in the fish, prefigures the burial and Resurrection (Matthew 12:40).”", "Say: “Melchizedek, the priest-king who brought out bread and wine for Abraham (Genesis 14), prefigures Christ the priest and the Eucharist. In every case, the fulfillment is greater than the type. Jesus is the true Lamb, true bread, true temple. Typology doesn’t make the Old Testament less real. It shows that God’s plan has one author and one center: Christ.”"],
    ask=["What is a type? (A person or event in the Old Testament that points to Christ.)", "What does the manna point to? (The Eucharist.)", "What does the bronze serpent point to? (The cross.)"],
    practice=["Prepare cards with types: Adam, Isaac, Joseph, Passover lamb, manna, bronze serpent, Jonah, Melchizedek. On other cards, put their fulfillments.", "Teams match the cards.", "Write one sentence for each pair: “Like ___, Jesus ___, but greater because ___.”", "Draw one pair on a sheet."],
    close="Pray: “Lord Jesus, you are the fulfillment of all the Scriptures.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Find a type of Christ in the Old Testament reading at Mass.”")),

'6-7-3': dict(
  saint="Ephrem the Deacon: a poet who taught the Church to sing its faith.",
  tryit=["Map a Sunday’s readings.", "Show how the Old Testament reading fits the Gospel.", "Write the connection in one sentence."],
  saintStory="Ephrem was born around 306 in Nisibis, in what is now Turkey, and lived in Syria. He became a deacon, never a priest, and taught in schools and wrote hymns in Syriac. In his time, false teachers were spreading their views in songs. Ephrem wrote orthodox hymns to the same tunes, and had choirs of women and men sing them.\nHis hymns are full of images from Scripture. He wrote about the Virgin Mary, the cross, Paradise and the Eucharist. He called Christ the “medicine of life,” and likened the Church to Noah’s ark and a harp that the Spirit plays.\nWhen the city of Edessa was struck by famine, he organized aid for the poor. He died in 373. He is called the “Harp of the Spirit” and a Doctor of the Church. Ephrem shows how Scripture and worship are woven together.",
  script=dict(
    open="Pass out a sheet with a few lines from a recent Sunday’s first reading and Gospel. Ask students to underline any word or image that appears in both. Share what they find.",
    teach=["Say: “At Sunday Mass there are usually three readings plus a psalm. The first reading is usually from the Old Testament (during Easter, from Acts). The responsorial psalm responds to the first reading. The second reading is from the letters or Revelation. The Gospel is the high point. The first reading is chosen to fit the Gospel. The Old Testament prepares for Christ and the Gospel reveals him. This follows what Jesus himself did on the road to Emmaus.”", "Say: “The readings follow a three-year cycle for Sundays: Year A (Matthew), Year B (Mark, with John in the summer), and Year C (Luke). Weekdays have a two-year cycle for the first reading. Over the cycle, the Church reads most of the Bible. The Church year also shapes the readings: Advent looks to the coming of the Savior, and the prophets (especially Isaiah) are read; Lent recalls the story of salvation, and the Easter Vigil reads from Genesis, Exodus and the prophets.”", "Say: “The Old Testament is also found throughout the prayers. The responsorial psalm, the Holy, Holy, Holy (Isaiah 6), the Lamb of God (Exodus 12 and John 1:29), the Our Father, and the mention of Abel, Abraham and Melchizedek in the Eucharistic Prayer all come from it. The Easter proclamation, the Exsultet, sings of Passover and of Adam’s ‘happy fault.’ When we understand the Old Testament, we pray the Mass better.”"],
    ask=["Why is the first reading chosen? (It fits the Gospel.)", "How many years is the Sunday cycle? (Three: A, B and C.)", "Name an Old Testament prayer we use at Mass. (Holy Holy Holy, Psalms, Lamb of God.)"],
    practice=["Give each pair the readings for a recent or upcoming Sunday.", "Read the first reading and the Gospel.", "Write a sentence that shows how the first reading prepares for the Gospel.", "Share, and then look for the type (like manna and the bread of life)."],
    close="Pray the psalm from the Sunday readings together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read the first reading and the Gospel before Mass.”")),

'6-8-1': dict(
  saint="Maximilian Kolbe: a friar who took the place of a condemned man.",
  tryit=["Rewrite each of the commandments as a positive.", "Add a modern example.", "Choose one to practice."],
  saintStory="Raymond Kolbe was born in Poland in 1894. As a boy he had a vision in which Mary offered him two crowns, white for purity and red for martyrdom, and he said he would take both. He joined the Franciscans, taking the name Maximilian, and started a movement and a magazine to honor Mary. He went to Japan as a missionary and founded a friary in Nagasaki.\nIn World War II, he was arrested by the Nazis for hiding Jews and Poles in his friary. He was sent to Auschwitz. In July 1941, a prisoner escaped, and the camp commander chose ten men to be starved to death in revenge. One of them, Franciszek Gajowniczek, cried out for his wife and children.\nFather Kolbe stepped forward and said, “I am a Catholic priest. I wish to take his place.” He led the men in prayer in the starvation bunker for two weeks, and was finally killed with an injection on August 14, 1941. Gajowniczek survived and lived into his nineties. Kolbe was named a saint in 1982.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Which is harder: to not do something wrong, or to do something good? Which is the commandment’s aim?”",
    teach=["Say: “The commandments can sound like a list of ‘don’ts.’ But each protects something precious, and Jesus shows what they ask of us positively. The Catechism teaches that each commandment says ‘no’ to something in order to say ‘yes’ to love. The first three: no other gods means put God first and worship him. Do not take his name in vain means treat God’s name with reverence. Keep the Sabbath means set time for God each week. Honor your father and mother means respect and gratitude toward family and legitimate authority.”", "Say: “The fifth commandment, do not kill, protects the dignity of every human life from conception to natural death, and also asks us to avoid hatred and to build peace. The sixth, do not commit adultery, protects marriage, faithfulness and purity. The seventh, do not steal, protects property and asks us to share and be honest in work. The eighth, do not bear false witness, protects the truth and others’ good name. The ninth and tenth, do not covet, guard the heart from envy and greed, since sin begins within.”", "Say: “Jesus said that the Law could be summed up in two commandments, love of God and love of neighbor. A teenager asked, ‘What good deed must I do to have eternal life?’ Jesus said, ‘If you would enter life, keep the commandments.’ (Matthew 19:17) The commandments are not outdated; they are the basic guide to how to love. Each one of us should know them, and use them in our examination of conscience before Confession.”"],
    ask=["What do the commandments protect? (God’s honor, life, family, truth, property and the heart.)", "What did Jesus say sums up the Law? (Love of God and love of neighbor.)", "How do we use the commandments? (In our examination of conscience.)"],
    practice=["Divide the class into ten groups. Each takes one commandment.", "They write on a poster: what it forbids, what it asks of us positively, and a modern example (including online life).", "Present to the class.", "Each student chooses one to practice this week."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, teach me your ways.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Use the commandments to examine your conscience tonight.”")),

'6-8-2': dict(
  saint="Dorothy Day: a journalist who founded the Catholic Worker and fed the hungry.",
  tryit=["Match a prophet’s message to a modern situation.", "Distinguish justice from mercy.", "Plan one action."],
  saintStory="Dorothy Day was born in 1897 in Brooklyn, New York. She became a journalist and a radical, writing for causes of the poor and workers. She was jailed for demonstrating for women’s right to vote. Though she was not religious, she was drawn to the Catholic Church by the faith of poor immigrants, and she was baptized in 1927 after the birth of her daughter.\nShe asked God to show her how to use her gifts for the poor. In 1933, with Peter Maurin, she started a newspaper, the Catholic Worker, which sold for a penny, and opened houses of hospitality in New York where people could find food and shelter. Soon there were houses across the country. She lived among the poor.\nShe was a pacifist and was arrested many times for protests. She prayed daily, went to Mass often and made retreats. She died in 1980. Her cause for canonization has been opened. She said, “I really only love God as much as I love the person I love the least.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up two bowls, one full and one empty. Say, “One person gets a full bowl, one gets nothing. Is it fair? Is it kind? Is fair the same as kind?” Take answers and let students argue the difference.",
    teach=["Say: “The prophets cared about justice. Amos said, ‘Let justice roll down like waters, and righteousness like an ever-flowing stream.’ (Amos 5:24) They spoke against cheating the poor, bribing judges, mistreating widows, orphans and foreigners. Micah 6:8 says, ‘What does the Lord require of you but to do justice, and to love kindness, and to walk humbly with your God?’ These come together.”", "Say: “Justice means giving each person what is due, especially basic rights: food, shelter, work and dignity. Mercy goes beyond that: it meets need with compassion. We need both. Charity gives food to the hungry; justice asks why people are hungry. The Church teaches the preferential option for the poor: care for those in need comes first. Catholic social teaching has principles such as the dignity of the human person, the common good, solidarity, and care for creation.”", "Say: “Jesus in the synagogue of Nazareth read from Isaiah: ‘He has anointed me to bring good news to the poor… to proclaim release to the captives… to set at liberty those who are oppressed.’ (Luke 4:18) This is his mission. The Church continues it. And Jesus said that we will be judged by how we treated the least of his brothers and sisters. The Old Testament’s call for justice has not ended. The Law of Moses even had provisions for the poor: leaving some of the harvest for them to glean (Leviticus 19:9-10), and the Jubilee year.”"],
    ask=["What does the prophet Micah say God requires? (Do justice, love kindness, walk humbly with God.)", "What is the difference between justice and mercy? (Justice gives what is due; mercy goes beyond with compassion.)", "What is one principle of Catholic social teaching? (Dignity of the person, common good, solidarity, option for the poor.)"],
    practice=["Give small groups a short situation: a classmate has no lunch; a neighbor is evicted; a factory pays unfair wages.", "For each, name what mercy would do now and what justice would ask about the cause.", "Choose one real need in your school or town and plan one action.", "Share your plan."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, make me an instrument of your peace and justice.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Do one act of mercy this week.”")),

'6-8-3': dict(
  saint="Bernard of Clairvaux: a monk who prayed the psalms every day and wrote about the love of God.",
  tryit=["Pray a psalm in the four steps.", "Write what each step gave you.", "Choose a psalm for the week."],
  saintStory="Bernard was born around 1090 to a noble family in Burgundy, France. When he was twenty-two, he persuaded thirty relatives and friends to join him at the poor monastery of Cîteaux. Within three years he was sent to found a new monastery at Clairvaux, which grew to hundreds of monks. He lived very strictly and became ill.\nHe prayed the psalms every day in the monastic Hours, and he wrote eighty-six sermons on the Song of Songs, describing the soul’s love for God. He loved the psalms for their depth and for the way they join our experience to God’s word. He wrote, “The measure of love is to love without measure.”\nBernard advised popes and kings, but remained a monk. He died in 1153. He shows what praying the psalms can do: join us to Christ’s own prayer.",
  script=dict(
    open="Play a short recording of a psalm being chanted, or read one aloud slowly. Ask, “What mood does this put you in? What would you say to God if you could not find words of your own?”",
    teach=["Say: “Because Jesus prayed the psalms, and the Church has prayed them for two thousand years, praying them joins us to Christ’s own voice. The Catechism says the psalms are the masterwork of prayer in the Old Testament (CCC 2585). We can pray them aloud or silently, alone or together. The Church also teaches that when we pray a psalm, Christ prays in us and with us.”", "Say: “Here is a way to pray a psalm slowly, in four steps. First, read the psalm slowly, aloud or silently. Second, meditate: stop at a word or line that stands out. Repeat it. What does it mean? Third, pray: answer God. Thank him, ask, say sorry, or simply stay with him. Fourth, rest: be still for a minute, and let God act. This follows the monastic tradition called lectio divina, sacred reading, which Bernard and the monks practiced.”", "Say: “You can match a psalm to your mood. Afraid: Psalm 23 or 91. Sorry: Psalm 51 or 130. Thankful: Psalm 100 or 136. Angry or sad: Psalm 13 or 42. Amazed by creation: Psalm 8 or 104. In the evening: Psalm 4 or 141. Many psalms end with praise even when they begin with pain. Learning even a few by heart, and praying them regularly, gives us words for every season of life.”"],
    ask=["Why do we pray the psalms? (Jesus prayed them; the Church prays them; they cover every feeling.)", "What are the four steps? (Read, meditate, pray, rest.)", "Which psalm could you pray when you are afraid? (Psalm 23 or 91.)"],
    practice=["Give each student Psalm 23 or 130 on a sheet.", "Guide them through the four steps: read slowly, underline a line, write a prayer, then sit in silence for one minute.", "Share one line that stood out.", "Choose a psalm to pray each day this week."],
    close="Pray Psalm 23 together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray a psalm before you sleep.”")),

'6-9-1': dict(
  saint="Thomas More: a lawyer and chancellor who put God before the king.",
  tryit=["List modern idols.", "Sort them by what they promise.", "Write the first commandment as a prayer."],
  saintStory="Thomas More was born in London in 1478. He became a lawyer, a scholar and a friend of Erasmus, and wrote a book called Utopia. He married, had four children, and filled his house with learning. He rose to be Lord Chancellor of England, the king’s highest official, in 1529.\nKing Henry VIII wanted to end his marriage to Catherine of Aragon, and when the pope refused, he broke with Rome and declared himself head of the Church in England. Thomas resigned as chancellor. He refused to sign an oath accepting the king as head of the Church, and was imprisoned in the Tower of London for over a year. His daughter Margaret visited him.\nAt his trial, he was condemned. On the scaffold, in 1535, he said that he died “the king’s good servant, but God’s first.” He was named a saint in 1935.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If a stranger looked at your calendar, phone and room, what would they say you love most?” Let students think quietly. “That is a good test of what we worship.”",
    teach=["Read Exodus 20:1-6. Say: “‘I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage. You shall have no other gods before me.’ The first commandment says that God alone is to be adored. We owe him faith, hope, love and worship. Jesus said, ‘You shall worship the Lord your God and him only shall you serve.’ (Matthew 4:10)”", "Say: “Ancient peoples carved statues. Today our idols may be money, popularity, sports, screens, success, power or pleasure. An idol is anything we put in the place of God, anything we trust to save us. Good things become idols when we make them ultimate. Sins against the first commandment include superstition, such as trusting horoscopes, charms or fortune-telling; the occult and attempts to contact spirits; sacrilege, which is disrespect for holy things; and atheism or indifference, ignoring God. Faith is the response to this commandment.”", "Say: “What does the first commandment ask? To believe in God, hope in him, love him with all our heart, and worship him in prayer and in the Mass. It’s not just a ban. It’s an invitation to the best thing in life. Augustine prayed, ‘You have made us for yourself, O Lord, and our hearts are restless until they rest in you.’ Thomas More shows that sometimes to keep this commandment takes courage.”"],
    ask=["What does the first commandment ask? (To worship God alone and love him above all.)", "What is an idol? (Anything we put in the place of God.)", "Name a sin against the first commandment. (Superstition, the occult, sacrilege, atheism, indifference.)"],
    practice=["Make a class list of modern idols.", "Sort them under: “promises happiness,” “promises safety,” “promises approval.”", "Write beside each: What does God offer instead?", "Rewrite the first commandment as a prayer in your own words."],
    close="Pray the Act of Faith or: “My Lord and my God.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Put God first today in one choice.”")),

'6-9-2': dict(
  saint="Bernardine of Siena: a preacher who taught people to honor the name of Jesus.",
  tryit=["Sort ways of using God’s name.", "Say which honor it and which dishonor it.", "Write a prayer of the Holy Name."],
  saintStory="Bernardine was born in 1380 in Tuscany, Italy. He was orphaned young and cared for by his aunt. As a young man, when the plague struck Siena, he helped nurse the sick at the hospital for months and almost died. He then joined the Franciscans in 1402.\nBernardine became a famous preacher. Huge crowds came to hear him, so many that he had to preach in open squares. He spoke in everyday language, with humor and stories, against gambling, vanity, feuds and cursing. He urged people to settle disputes and make peace.\nHe promoted devotion to the holy name of Jesus. He would hold up a tablet with the letters IHS, the first letters of Jesus’ name in Greek, surrounded by rays of light, and people would kneel. He died in 1444.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say a student’s name with respect, then with contempt. Ask, “What changed? A name stands for the person. What about God’s name?”",
    teach=["Read Exodus 20:7. Say: “‘You shall not take the name of the Lord your God in vain.’ God’s name is holy. In the Bible, a name is not just a label but a person’s identity. When God revealed his name to Moses, it was a gift of closeness, and the Jews are so reverent that they will not say it. The second commandment calls us to honor God’s name, and the names of Jesus, Mary and the saints.”", "Say: “What breaks this commandment? Blasphemy, which is speaking against God, Jesus, Mary or the saints with contempt. Cursing, which is calling down evil in God’s name. Using God’s name casually, such as ‘Oh my God’ as an exclamation. False oaths: swearing to something false, or breaking a promise made in God’s name. Jesus said, ‘Let what you say be simply Yes or No.’ (Matthew 5:37) Oaths are allowed when needed, such as in court, but must be truthful. Also, using God’s name to justify violence or hatred.”", "Say: “What honors the name? Praying it with reverence. Making the Sign of the Cross, which begins in the name of the Father, Son and Holy Spirit. Bowing our head at the name of Jesus. Praising: ‘Blessed be the name of the Lord.’ The Jesus Prayer: ‘Lord Jesus Christ, Son of God, have mercy on me, a sinner.’ Paul writes, ‘At the name of Jesus every knee should bow.’ (Philippians 2:10) The name Jesus means ‘God saves.’ Our own baptismal name is also holy: we are baptized in the name of the Trinity and carry the name Christian.”"],
    ask=["What does the second commandment ask? (To honor and respect God’s name.)", "What are some ways it is broken? (Blasphemy, cursing, careless use, false oaths.)", "What does the name Jesus mean? (God saves.)"],
    practice=["Write ways of using God’s name on cards: “Praying the Our Father,” “Saying OMG as a joke,” “Making a promise before God,” “Cursing,” “Bowing at the name of Jesus,” “Lying under oath,” “Praising.”", "Sort into two piles: honors the name or dishonors it.", "Talk about how to replace careless words.", "Write a short prayer to the holy name, such as the Jesus Prayer."],
    close="Pray the Jesus Prayer five times slowly. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Notice how you use God’s name this week and bow at the name of Jesus at Mass.”")),

'6-9-3': dict(
  saint="Isidore the Farmer: a laborer who began every day at Mass.",
  tryit=["Plan a Lord’s Day.", "Include Mass, rest, family and service.", "Mark what you will give up."],
  saintStory="Isidore was born around 1070 in Madrid, Spain, to poor parents. He worked as a farm laborer on the estate of a wealthy landowner, Juan de Vargas, and spent his life in the same fields. He married a woman named Maria, who is also honored as a saint, and they had a son who died young.\nIsidore went to Mass before work each morning, and prayed as he plowed. Some of the other workers complained to the landowner that Isidore was arriving late. When Juan went to look, he saw an angel plowing alongside Isidore, according to the old story.\nIsidore was generous with the little he had, sharing his food with the poor and even feeding the birds in winter. He died in 1130 and was named a saint in 1622. He is the patron of farmers. His life shows how to put God first in a day of work.",
  script=dict(
    open="Draw a simple weekly calendar on the board and ask students to fill in a typical Sunday. Notice what takes the most room. Ask, “What would make this a day that feels different from the other six?”",
    teach=["Read Exodus 20:8-11 and Genesis 2:2-3. Say: “‘Remember the Sabbath day, to keep it holy. Six days you shall labor… but the seventh day is a Sabbath to the Lord your God.’ God rested after creation, blessed the seventh day and made it holy. The Sabbath is a gift, a day for worship, family and rest. The Israelites were slaves in Egypt who never had a day off. God made them free, and gave them a day of rest.”", "Say: “For Christians, the Lord’s Day is Sunday, the day of the Resurrection. It is the first day of the week and also the ‘eighth day’ of new creation. The Church obliges Catholics to attend Mass on Sundays and holy days of obligation, and to avoid unnecessary work. This is a serious obligation; missing Mass without a good reason (like illness) is a grave sin. Mass is where we meet the risen Lord, hear his word and receive his Body. The early Christians said, ‘We cannot live without Sunday.’”", "Say: “Sunday is also for rest, family and joy: time with family and friends, meals, play, visits to the sick, acts of charity, time in nature, quiet reading and prayer. Jesus said, ‘The Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath.’ (Mark 2:27) Rest is not wasted time; it is a sign that we are not slaves to work and productivity. Today it is easy to fill Sunday with activities and screens. A holy Sunday takes planning. Isidore went to Mass first, then worked, and trusted God to provide.”"],
    ask=["Why do Christians keep Sunday? (The day of the Resurrection.)", "What does the Church require on Sundays? (Mass and rest from unnecessary work.)", "Is rest wasted time? (No, it is a gift and a sign of trust in God.)"],
    practice=["Draw a plan for a Lord’s Day: morning, Mass, meal, afternoon, evening.", "Include worship (Mass, prayer), rest (play, quiet), family (a meal, a game), and service (visit or call someone).", "Mark one thing you will give up on Sunday to make it special, such as chores, extra screen time or shopping.", "Share your plan with a partner."],
    close="Pray: “This is the day the Lord has made; let us rejoice and be glad in it.” (Psalm 118:24.) Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Make a family Sunday plan.”")),
}
